.spacing 1
.c
Comparison of ADA Environment Requirements
.c
with UCI Lisp Environment Facilities
.blank 2
.c
Stephen Willson
.c
January 21, 1981
.p 5
This informal paper first compares the ADA environment requirements
mentioned in class with the features available in UCI Lisp, and
then discusses how those currently available features could help
us to implement the desired features for an ADA environment,
and the impact of such an approach on the design of the ADA interpreter.
.list 1
.le
Run a program or execute an expression at the top level.  This facility
exists.
.le
Suspend a running program (_^C).  This facility exists.
.le
Indicate where suspended.  This facility exists, though not exactly in
as pleasant a form as is desired.
.le
At point of suspension:
.list 1
.le
Look at variables.  This facility exists.
.le
Perform assignments.  This facility exists.
.le
Call a subprogram.  This facility exists.
.le
Resume execution.  This facility exists.
.le
Return a value from a function (exiting the function).  This facility exists.
.le
Transfer control via GOTO.  This facility not available.
.le
Exit a loop.  This facility not available.
.end list
.le
Call [structure] Editor to edit a granule.  This facility exists.
.le
Allow breakpoints and tracing.  This facility exists.  "Freeze universe on
condition."##This facility not available.
.le
Call file package (input/output/directories).  This facility exists.
.le
Single Step.  This facility not readily available.           
.le
Sending messages.  This facility not available.
.le
Getting help.  This facility not available.
.end list
.p 5
Possiblities for using current UCI Lisp environment capablities to
create ADA environment desired:
.list 1
.le
If we alter the (print (eval (read))) loop to use our version of read
always, then at any point that the user may wish to enter an ADA form
he may, without having to worry about messing up the Lisp scanner.
We can also enforce this use of our read routine by setting initfn to
be our own evaluation loop.  This means that if a disaster happens while
an ADA user is using the ADA environment, he won't suddenly wind up
in the Lisp environment.  This solves the "((worker))" problem as
mentioned in class (Eliza).
.le
If we binding mechanism similar to Lisp's, namely by keeping
all visible objects and functions on visible property lists and
all hidden objects and functions stored in a dynamic stack, then (perhaps)
the work of implementing the functionality desired during breaks can
be simplified.
.b
Lisp has hooks for allowing the break package to be called.  If our
interpreter is rolling along and wants to handle a break, then
our interpreter could call the Lisp break package.  This package
has hooks for allowing us to enter our own functions that can
modify the run-time stack and variable bindings in the ADA interpreter.
This, combined with the use of our own read routine, would hopefully
allow us to implement an ADA break package that would give us the
added benefit of being potentially able to execute the Lisp
break package, thus facilitating the debugging of our system.
.le
If desired, a facility for getting help and sending messages could
be added by adding some macro-10 code to manipulate a pseudo-tty.
Suppose I want to send a message.  I could type _^C_^B, enter the Lisp
break package that had been augmented with our special features,
send the message via a pty without harming my current environment,
and then continue execution of my ADA program.  The same scheme
could likewise be used to get help.
.le
Single stepping could simply be an option to the ADA interpreter.
.le
Calls to the file package would be the same as being able to
execute an arbitrary (visible) function during a break.  If we use
our own read routine, then the user could simply enter the call
and have it executed immediately.  Likewise for calling the Editor.
The Editor would not require extensive knowledge of the internal
structures used by the interpreter if the visible functions are stored
on property lists.
.end list
.p 5
Summary:
.b
By writing the ADA interpreter in such a way that it uses 
.list 1
.le
property
lists to store the visible objects and functions,
.le
has hooks available via the break package for manipulation during breaks,
.le
and enforcing the use of our own read routine
.end list
.b
it will be possible to use the features already present in Lisp to
.list 1
.le
debug our interpreter,
.le
allow addition of "neat" environment features like messages and help,
.le
give us a flexible framework for adding new features (via break macros),
.le
and allow us a great deal of control over the characters the user might
type (including _^C interrupts).
.end list
